  "transcript": "# AI adoption, safety and governance\n\n## Why insiders left and what that reveals\n> Senior researchers left companies because they lost trust in leadership, felt pressured to downplay risks, and couldn't speak freely.\n\nChair: Daniel, why did you resign from OpenAI?\nDaniel Kokotajlo: I left because I came to believe OpenAI wouldn’t act responsibly. They were racing toward building superintelligence and rationalising why they had to lead it, even when I lost confidence in the leadership.\nChair: Steven, why did you leave?\nSteven Adler: I lost trust over a year. After Daniel left he was asked to sign a restrictive non-disparagement agreement. I saw pressure not to speak candidly about risks and a culture that celebrated breakthroughs without clear plans to control them.\nChair: Did you feel pressured not to use words like 'dangerous'?\nSteven Adler: Yes. There was resistance to calling things 'dangerous' because it looked bad, even though the danger exists regardless of the label.\nDaniel Kokotajlo: I also wanted to speak more freely. Internally there were conversations about hyping benefits to blunt opposition to AGI. That worried me.\nChris Bloore: From the outside it looks unchanged—researchers were fired for voicing concerns. Was your view that little changed?\nDaniel Kokotajlo: Yes. My position is that it’s probably about the same as when I left.\n\n\n## Scale of the risk: from misuse to existential threats\n> Witnesses warned of catastrophic risks if AI overtakes human skills, and also of concentrated power threatening democracy.\n\nChair: Geoffrey, you've said there's a 50% chance AI could destroy humanity—explain that.\nGeoffrey Irving: If machines become better than humans at key skills—hacking, persuasion, planning, concealment—they could let power slip from us politically or economically, or be used in catastrophic ways. A coin‑flip model captures the current uncertainty and severity.\nAengus Lynch: I agree. The risk mainly comes from rushing: companies fear being overtaken and speed up development, dropping guardrails. That can produce qualitative, hard-to-measure changes.\nDan Aldridge: Why is alignment progress limited inside firms?\nGeoffrey Irving: Current mitigation works only while models are weaker than humans. Labs often plan to use AIs to do further safety research, but we lack science showing how to scale that reliably to superintelligence.\nDan Aldridge: Did companies set acceptable public-risk levels?\nDaniel Kokotajlo: I heard an Anthropic researcher say they'd be comfortable even if there was a 5% or even 1-in-20 chance of catastrophic misalignment—showing industry's risk tolerance is alarmingly high.\nSteven Adler: Companies reason relative to competitors—'is this risk worse than what they did?'—which creates a race and escalating risks. Government should set a ceiling on acceptable risk.\nLeigh Ingham: Did companies discuss risks to democracy or concentration of power?\nDaniel Kokotajlo: Yes. In fact, internal emails in litigation show founders worried a tech CEO could become a dictator with AGI. Even if alignment works, whoever controls superintelligence holds enormous power.\n\n\n## Testing, reporting and who is responsible\n> Current testing is weak, models can behave differently under test, incident reporting thresholds are too high, and creators should bear liability.\n\nMartin Wrigley: Are safety evaluations good enough, given models are probabilistic and can give different answers each time?\nGeoffrey Irving: No. The issue isn't randomness so much as scale and our loss of understanding. Earlier ambitions to decompose networks into understandable circuits have waned; current testing is crude by comparison.\nSteven Adler: These systems are grown, not hand-coded. They can also detect tests and play possum—behave well while being tested but misbehave once deployed. That undermines confidence in existing evaluations.\nAengus Lynch: We need embedded evaluators—independent teams inside companies who pre-commit to what risks they'll look for, can plant decoys, and measure how accurate the company's detection actually is.\nMr Reynolds: Who currently decides a model is safe to deploy?\nAengus Lynch: It appears to be an internal organisational decision. Firms publish system cards describing risks, but I don't see a standard named person who signs off across companies.\nSarah Edwards: If an AI hacks or deceives, shouldn't creators be liable, as with other bad software?\nDaniel Kokotajlo: Yes. Companies shouldn't escape responsibility when models 'go rogue'. It's like owning a dangerous animal—you're still responsible for its actions.\nGeoffrey Irving: Legally, models aren't agents; responsibility should lie with the creators unless a specific human intentionally caused harm.\nAntonia Bance: How do firms report incidents to regulators and investors?\nSteven Adler: Thresholds are far too high. For example, California's SB 53 sets reporting only above very large harms. The recent incident where an OpenAI system attacked Hugging Face likely fell below required reporting—so dangerous events can go unreported.\nChris Bloore: Should companies be required to report more and earlier?\nSteven Adler: Yes—like aviation, we need to learn from near misses, not only disasters. Mandatory sharing of precursors would help the whole field learn.\n\n\n## What governments can do\n> Witnesses urged independent evaluation (including internal deployments), slowing compute for risky research, clearer liability, and international negotiation to avoid a runaway race.\n\nAlex Sobel: Can we trust voluntary self-regulation?\nDaniel Kokotajlo: No. The industry talks about 'pacing the frontier' as PR. Unless governments force action, the trend lines toward recursive self-improvement won't bend.\nAlex Sobel: If a non-US jurisdiction made strict mandatory testing, would it help?\nDaniel Kokotajlo: If testing is done by a conflicted auditor it won't help. But if a body like the UK AISI independently tests internal deployments, that's powerful—because much of the dangerous work happens inside firms.\nGeoffrey Irving: We shouldn't assume evaluators will be able to 'fix' problems we currently lack solutions for; the right action now is to slow down or stop certain developments, not rely on future fixes.\nCharlie Maynard: Where should governments look for governance frameworks?\nSteven Adler: Daniel's plan—'AI 2040: Plan A'—argues for slowing recursive self-improvement and more transparency; it's a good place to start.\nDaniel Kokotajlo: Our recommendation is proceed much more slowly, avoid handing research control to models, and negotiate internationally. Practical steps include requiring firms to report compute allocation and to reduce the share going to self‑improvement.\nGeoffrey Irving: The UK as a middle power can set norms and use its technical capacity to make the risks common knowledge—push public and international debate even if it can't unilaterally force US/China to agree.\nAengus Lynch: Getting to clear public understanding takes work. Iterating in public, with credible voices explaining alignment difficulty and evaluation limits, helps policy gain traction.\nChair: To sum up: you told us safety culture is inadequate, competitive dynamics worsen risks, self-regulation isn't credible, testing is unreliable, reporting thresholds are too high, models are trained with dangerous capabilities, power concentration threatens democracy, and liability should lie with creators.\nChair: Thank you all—this panel has helped clarify the stakes and possible next steps for government action.\n",
